f needed.\" So sizing starts from reference values — branch epics don't need to wait for benchmark; adjustment is incremental. No edge; note in branch ACs: \"starting values from model-registry-operator reference; adjusted per benchmark findings\". Good, keeps parallelism.\n\nWait, actually — should E003 (benchmark) be an Investigation instead? It \"resolves uncertainty\" (is pgvector in same order of magnitude?) but produces a deliverable (benchmark report + sizing). The type definition: Investigation = resolve uncertainty (answer a question or make a decision that other epics depend on); Implementation = produce an artifact. The benchmark produces an artifact (report, sizing recommendation) and per staff input the answer doesn't change approach (pgvector is decided; flat scan sufficient). So Implementation. Hmm, but \"verify pgvector is in the same order of magnitude\" — if it fails catastrophically, approach changes? Staff input already scoped it: not meeting a threshold isn't a failure; it's dev experimentation. If pgvector were 1000x slower it would matter... but the staff input forecloses that concern. I'll classify as Implementation (produces benchmark artifact + sizing recommendation). Component: Gen AI Studio. \n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: maybe make it an Investigation with the question \"is pgvector performance within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline for typical playground RAG usage\" — deliverable benchmark. The routing would then use investigation_signals. But it produces an artifact (benchmark harness + report) → Implementation is fine. I'll go Implementation.\n\n- Branch epics BRANCH-A-E003? Wait, numbering. The prompt example: E001 shared (Investigation), BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003... The branch epics get numbers like E004, E005 in sequence? Example shows {ID}-BRANCH-A-E003 and {ID}-BRANCH-B-E003 and {ID}-BRANCH-B-E004 — branch letter then epic number, where the number is the epic's position. So branch epics share numbering space with main plan? In the example, E001 is investigation, branch epics are E003+ (E002 maybe another shared epic). So branch epics numbered after main-plan epics. My main plan: E001 (Investigation), E002 (provider enablement), E003 (benchmark). Branch epics: BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004, BRANCH-C-E004 (provisioning per approach). \n\nWait — should branch epics depend on E002 as well? The provisioning work (creating PostgreSQL Deployment etc.) doesn't build against the provider wiring — they're different aspects of the same component (BFF). Rule 15: same repo different areas → parallel... but here BFF provisioning and BFF provider config are in the same component area, both in gen-ai BFF. Do they share surfaces? The provider wiring consumes the connection secret the provisioning creates — a configuration reference (connection string), not a build-against artifact. Per Rule 13's spirit: configuration references are AC gates, not edges. So branch epics depend only on E001 (gated_by). Their ACs include \"the provisioned instance's connection details are consumed by the playground's pgvector vector_io configuration\". Good.\n\nAnd E003 (benchmark) depends on E002 only.\n\nNow — what about ogx-distribution? Is there any OGX-side work? Per architecture context, ogx-distribution already ships remote PGVector provider support. Staff input: \"The existing remote vector store implementation can be largely reused for pgvector integration.\" So no ogx-distribution code epic. But validation of the OGX pgvector provider at the distribution level? That's covered by E003 benchmark (exercises the provider) and branch ACs (E2E). I'll note in the summary that ogx-distribution requires no code changes (passive component — existing remote pgvector provider), with validation folded into benchmark/E2E ACs. Actually wait — does the ogx-distribution image need the pymilvus/pgvector client libs? The arch context shows ogx-distribution supports PGVector remote backend already. And the strategy premise \"inline providers will be removed from the Llama Stack distribution image\" (RHAISTRAT-7474) — that's the other strategy's work. OK, passive.\n\nWhat about rhods-operator CSV (RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE)? Staff input: already present. No epic needed. For branch A (operator-based, model-registry pattern), the image reference would be needed — already exists. Note it.\n\nAuto Rag: removed from scope per staff input. Note override.\n\nDo we need a docs epic? The strategy doesn't say Documentation Support: Yes. Branch C (BYO) inherently includes documentation of configuration — as scope within that branch epic, not a standalone docs-authoring epic. No docs-authoring epic.\n\nNow priorities: strategy priority Blocker → P0 for the critical path. All epics here are essentially P0 (blocking RHAISTRAT-7474, GA-track capability). E001 P0 (blocks all). E002 P0. E003 (benchmark) — is it P0? It informs sizing; staff calls it sprint 1. The ACs it serves: \"lightweight enough to run in resource-constrained environments\". I'd say P1? Strategy has no HLR priority list (missing section). Frontmatter priority Blocker → the whole strategy is critical. Hmm. The benchmark validates the \"lightweight/order-of-magnitude\" AC → part of acceptance. I'll make E003 P1 (important validation but doesn't block the out-of-box capability; sizing starts from reference values). Hmm, but priority inheritance: prerequisite epic inherits highest priority of HLRs it transitively enables. Without HLR table, I'll derive from strategy ACs. The core ACs (out-of-box availability, RAG functions) map to E001/E002/branch epics → P0. The \"lightweight\" AC maps partly to E003 → I'll still make E003 P1 and note it. Actually — \"no loss of current capability\" is also validated by the benchmark (order-of-magnitude check). Ugh. Let me just make E003 P1 with rationale: validation/sizing work, not blocking delivery of the out-of-box capability; note as potential priority consideration. Ac