tion scope).\n- Derive recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference values; adjust based on findings.\n\nACs:\n- Benchmark report exists with inline Milvus baseline, pgvector results, and order-of-magnitude comparison for representative workloads.\n- Recommended resource requests/limits documented, referencing model-registry-operator values as starting point.\n- Report states flat-scan (no index) suitability for typical playground usage and positions the default store for development experimentation (production-scale workloads directed to external vector databases).\n\nHLR Traceability: AC-2, AC-6.\n\n**BRANCH-A-E004** (Implementation, branch A — operator-based):\nTitle: \"Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio (RHAISTRAT-7164 available)\"\n\nDescription: When the RHOAI-controlled operator layer is available (dashboard operator modularization delivered), implement PostgreSQL lifecycle management for the Gen AI Studio default vector store in that operator, following the model-registry-operator pattern. Uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included) — no custom image build or Konflux onboarding needed.\n\nScope:\n- Implement PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning and lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry this RHOAI-specific logic).\n- Follow the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioning pattern: per-scope PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, credentials Secret, and persistent storage, using the registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16 image (pgvector included); RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE is already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs.\n- Default scoping per the confirmed model (per-namespace preferred for isolation).\n- Resource requests/limits starting from model-registry-operator reference values, adjusted per benchmark findings.\n- E2E validation: out-of-box RAG in the playground with no manual setup, functioning RAG workflows with no loss of current capability, per-namespace isolation, separate process from OGX.\n\nACs:\n- A playground with RAG configured gets a working default vector store with zero manual provisioning steps.\n- PostgreSQL instance lifecycle (creation, deletion, credential management) is managed by the operator, scoped per the confirmed model with namespace isolation.\n- The provisioned database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server instance.\n- Resource requests/limits are set from the reference values and documented.\n- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function end-to-end with no loss of current capability (validated with the remote pgvector provider enabled).\n- Disconnected-install support: image reference resolvable via the existing related-image environment variable.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove, fallback=\"If the dashboard operator modularization does not deliver the RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time for RHOAI 3.5, this epic does not exist; the selected fallback is BFF-managed provisioning or customer-supplied PostgreSQL per the go/no-go decision.\"\n\n**BRANCH-B-E004** (branch B — BFF-managed):\nTitle: \"BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\"\n\nDescription: If the operator layer is not available in time, have the gen-ai BFF provision the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly, similar to how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. Designed for portability to the platform-provided shared database expected in RHOAI 3.6.\n\nScope:\n- BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and Secret in the namespace when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists.\n- Portability: consume a shared platform connection string when the DSC-managed shared PostgreSQL becomes available (3.6) instead of self-provisioning.\n- Explicit lifecycle design: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, credential rotation — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so these need explicit design attention.\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included); resource sizing from model-registry-operator reference values adjusted per benchmark findings; scoping per the confirmed model.\n- E2E validation of out-of-box RAG (same AC set as the operator-based branch).\n\nACs: (mirror of A, adjusted)\n- Playground with RAG configured → working default vector store, no manual steps.\n- PostgreSQL resources created/owned by the BFF with documented lifecycle behavior (creation, cleanup on playground deletion, restart handling, credential rotation).\n- Portability: connection consumption abstracted so a platform-provided shared connection string can replace self-provisioning without user-facing change.\n- Separate process, namespace isolation, resource limits, E2E no-loss-of-capability validation.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove, fallback=\"If the go/no-go decision selects operator-based provisioning or customer-supplied PostgreSQL, this epic is not implemented.\"\n\nHmm wait — gate_failure_impact semantics: \"what changes if outcome B does not hold\". For branch B, the outcome is \"operator layer not available AND BFF-managed chosen\". If not chosen → epic removed. OK.\n\n**BRANCH-C-E004** (branch C — customer-supplied):\nTitle: \"Customer-supplied PostgreSQL configuration support for Gen AI Studio RAG\"\n\nDescription: Degraded-mode fallback: require customers to bring their own PostgreSQL for RAG capabilities in the playground, configuring the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details. Aligns with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0.\n\nScope:\n- Support user-configured PostgreSQL connection details (connectionString secret) on the OGXServer CR for the pgvector vector_io provider.\n- BFF validation and clear error handling/messaging when no default vector store is available and none is configured.\n- User-facing documentation of the required OGXServer spec configuration for BYO PostgreSQL.\n- E2E validation that playground RAG functions against user-supplied PostgreSQL.\n\nACs:\n- A user-configured PostgreSQL connection on the OGXServer spec results in working playground RAG via pgvector.\n- Absence of a default and a user connection produces a clear, actionable error in the playground.\n- Documentation of the configuration steps is delivered and technically reviewed.\n- No manual steps beyond the documented configuration are required.\n\ngate_failure_impact: action=remove, fallback=\"If a self-provisioning approach (operator-based or BFF-managed) is selected, this epic is not implemented; its documentation scope may still be retained as user guidance.\"\n\nNow — the E2E validation \"no loss of capability\" AC appears in E002 as well (dev/test) and branch epics (E2E). Fine.\n\nSignal files. Write tables with Signal, Value, Rationale.\n\nNow the decomposition summary body. Sections: Epic List, Dependency DAG, DAG Justification, HLR Traceability Matrix, Health Warnings, Tiered Delivery. Plus Staff Engineer Input override log (Step 1.5 says log each override in the decomposition summary). I'll add a section \"Staff Engineer Input Overrides\".\n\nLet me now write all files. I'll write bodies with Write tool, then set frontmatter via script.\n\nCareful: Write tool creates files; frontmatter.py set adds frontmatter. Let me check how frontmatter.py set works on a file without frontmatter — presumably it prepends. The prompt says write body without frontmatter delimiters then set via script. OK.\n\nAlso careful with shell quoting for multi-word values. I'll use single-quoted strings in bash.\n\nLet me write the decomposition summary first.\n\nFor epic_count and critical_path_length in frontmatter: epic_count=6, critical_path_length=2.\n\nWait, critical path: main-plan critical path — E002→E003 is length 2. But branch chain E001→BRANCH-X-E004 is also 2. Branch epics excluded from main-plan critical path. So 2. Good.\n\nLet me write files now. Note: no triage fields (not triaged).\n\nOne more check on the \"no cross-references to sibling epics\" rule for the decomposition summary — the rule applies to epic bodies, not the summary (summary must use epic IDs in tables/DAG). Good.\n\nWriting E001 body — must avoid referencing \"E002\" etc. Also branch epics must not be referenced by main-plan epics. E001's body mentions \"conditional branches attached to this epic as a plan document\" — that's fine, no IDs.\n\nNow write.","signature":"1d69e5f76e4e40a98564a8b457a9f816"}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":77581,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85a45358e228944","agent_id":"a2b696fdb420d40ad","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"22ab07ce-8dbe-4275-98b2-30b6a4bd1969","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:28:08.257Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
