{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-fa603881-cee9-4165-a53b-2f989569b55d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1589b9eec3f4bab","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-hiqgx185/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Decide the default vector store provisioning approach for Gen AI Studio (go/no-go gate)\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves how the default pgvector-backed vector store is provisioned for the Gen AI Studio playground in RHOAI 3.5, and confirms the scoping model for that store. It produces a decision record that selects one of three bounded provisioning outcomes and defines the go/no-go gate criteria, decision date, and owner as part of sprint planning. The downstream provisioning implementation is conditional on this decision: which implementation exists, and what it does, is determined by the outcome.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Track and verify the delivery status of the dashboard operator modularization effort (RHAISTRAT-7164) against the RHOAI 3.5 release timeline. This is the hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning: the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic, so until the modularization delivers a RHOAI-controlled operator layer, there is no home for PostgreSQL lifecycle management.\n- Define the go/no-go gate: the criteria, the decision date, and the owner, as part of sprint planning.\n- Select the provisioning approach among the bounded outcomes:\n  - **A — operator-based self-provisioning**: PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer, following the model-registry-operator pattern (viable only if the operator layer lands in time).\n  - **B — BFF-managed provisioning**: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (as it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure), with portability and lifecycle design attention.\n  - **C — customer-supplied PostgreSQL**: users configure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details (degraded mode aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes).\n- Confirm the scoping model for the default vector store — per namespace versus per OGXServer instance — noting that per-namespace is preferred for isolation and that the per-instance consumer (Auto Rag) is out of scope for this strategy (its integration is targeted at RHOAI 3.6 under RHAISTRAT-7824).\n- Record the decision, rationale, and gate outcome in a decision record consumed by sprint planning; the conditional implementation branches for each outcome are attached to this epic as a plan document.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A decision record exists that names the selected provisioning approach, the go/no-go gate criteria, the decision date, and the owner.\n- The decision is grounded in the verified delivery status of the dashboard operator modularization against the RHOAI 3.5 timeline, not in assumption.\n- The scoping model (per-namespace or per-OGXServer-instance) is confirmed with rationale, consistent with multi-tenant isolation.\n- Each bounded outcome identifies the implementation work it selects, so the conditional branches are unambiguous.\n- The decision is socialized with sprint planning and the Gen AI Studio stakeholders.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\" — this epic is the prerequisite decision that determines how that capability is delivered (bundled, not independently deferrable: it shares the same deliverable surface and has no standalone user value).\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation)\" — the scoping model is resolved here.\n- Strategy open question \"What is the preferred scoping model — per namespace, per Llama Stack instance, or both?\" — explicitly resolved by this epic.\n- Staff Engineer Input: operator dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164, fallback definition, and the directive that the development team define a go/no-go gate as part of sprint planning.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":89691,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85a45358e228944","agent_id":"a2b696fdb420d40ad","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"9c66729f-82d7-409f-b379-78e11a08b9ad","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:29:07.698Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
