{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-77914c25-a57b-4fd0-be15-fa3339e1feac","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9accf76da711577e","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-hiqgx185/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B)\n\n## Description\n\nConditional branch B of the provisioning decision: implemented when the go/no-go gate determines that the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (from the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) will not be available in time for RHOAI 3.5 and selects BFF-managed provisioning as the fallback. The odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly — similar to how it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure — creating the Kubernetes resources for the default vector store when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace. The implementation is designed for portability to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL expected in RHOAI 3.6, and gives explicit design attention to lifecycle concerns because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed provisioning: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, persistent volume claim, and credentials Secret, using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included — no custom image build needed).\n- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning; the connection-consumption path is abstracted so this switch requires no user-facing change.\n- Address lifecycle management explicitly, since the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation each get a documented design and implementation.\n- Scope the provisioned instance per the confirmed scoping model (per-namespace is the preferred default for multi-tenant isolation).\n- Set PostgreSQL resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's reference values, adjusted per the sprint-1 benchmark findings when available.\n- End-to-end validation of the out-of-box experience: a playground with RAG configured gets a working default vector store with zero manual steps, and RAG workflows function with no loss of current capability with the remote pgvector provider enabled.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A Gen AI Studio playground configured with RAG capabilities gets a working default pgvector vector store with zero manual provisioning or configuration steps by the user, via BFF-created Kubernetes resources.\n- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and documented: resources are cleaned up on playground deletion, PostgreSQL pod restarts are handled, and credential rotation is designed and tested.\n- The connection-consumption path is abstracted so a platform-provided shared connection string can replace self-provisioning without user-facing change (portability verified by a test that swaps the connection source).\n- The provisioned PostgreSQL runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server instance, scoped per the confirmed model with per-namespace isolation.\n- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference values and documented, with benchmark-driven adjustments noted.\n- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function end-to-end with the remote pgvector vector_io provider enabled, with no loss of current capability.\n- The provisioned instance's connection details are consumed by the playground's pgvector vector_io configuration via a Kubernetes secret.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\" — this epic is the BFF-managed delivery of that capability under branch outcome B.\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability\" — end-to-end validation included in this epic's scope.\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider)\".\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation)\".\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is lightweight enough to run in resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead\" — resource requests/limits applied to the provisioned instance (bundled: the sizing is applied on the same provisioning surface this epic owns; splitting it would require a second pass over the same created resources).\n- Staff Engineer Input: alternative fallback (BFF-managed provisioning following the guardrails-infrastructure pattern), portability caveat for the 3.6 shared database, lifecycle caveats (cleanup, restarts, credential rotation), existing pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL image, resource sizing reference.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":93320,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85a45358e228944","agent_id":"a2b696fdb420d40ad","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"f915bb12-bc57-4fef-8ad1-efbb42ce6b91","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:29:57.669Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
