{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-98a73301d931602c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E003\n3\ttitle: Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource\n4\t  sizing\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P1\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n12\tai_signals:\n13\t  change_specificity: 1\n14\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n15\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n16\t  existing_foundation: 1\n17\t  open_questions: 1\n18\t  external_dependency: 0\n19\t  human_process_gates: 0\n20\t  repo_access: 1\n21\t  architecture_claims: 1\n22\timplementation_type: null\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: High\n25\tai_implementability_score: 6\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tSprint-1 benchmark that captures the inline Milvus provider baseline and verifies that pgvector performs in the same order of magnitude for typical Gen AI Studio playground RAG usage, plus a recommended resource sizing for the default PostgreSQL/pgvector instance. The default store is positioned for development experimentation, not production usage: strict latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99) are not required, and typical playground RAG usage involves small document sets where a flat scan (no index) is sufficient. The benchmark runs against a self-provisioned dev/test pgvector instance and does not depend on the production provisioning mechanism selected for the platform.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\t- Stand up a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL instance in a development/test environment using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector is included out of the box — no custom image build is needed).\n40\t- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline for representative playground RAG workloads (small document sets, insertion and retrieval).\n41\t- Run the same workloads against pgvector with flat scan (no index) and compare results.\n42\t- Produce a benchmark report stating the order-of-magnitude comparison — not a pass/fail against specific latency thresholds — and confirming flat-scan suitability for typical playground usage.\n43\t- Derive recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits for the default instance, starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits as the reference values, adjusted based on the benchmark findings.\n44\t- Position the default store in the report: development experimentation scope, with users holding production-scale vector workloads directed to an external vector database.\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- A benchmark report exists that documents the inline Milvus baseline measurements, the pgvector measurements, and an order-of-magnitude comparison for representative playground RAG workloads.\n49\t- The report confirms (or flags) that flat scan over small document sets is sufficient for the default store's intended usage.\n50\t- Recommended PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are documented, explicitly referencing the model-registry-operator values as the starting point and the benchmark-driven adjustments.\n51\t- The report states the positioning of the default store (development experimentation; production-scale workloads should use an external vector database).\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability\n54\t\n55\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability\" — the order-of-magnitude comparison against the inline Milvus baseline is the measurable form of \"no loss\" for dev-experimentation scope.\n56\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is lightweight enough to run in resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead\" — the sizing recommendation derived here.\n57\t- Staff Engineer Input: sprint-1 benchmark scope (baseline capture, order of magnitude, no thresholds, flat scan) and PostgreSQL resource sizing direction (model-registry-operator reference values, adjusted per benchmarking).\n58\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8f8f198478e2793","agent_id":"a9f3dec29b468d8b4","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"c239be2c-5c09-4f67-8193-c913b520f72f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:33:07.256Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-98a73301d931602c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
