{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8824374be6b0ff4f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n4\t  (branch B)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the go/no-go decision selects operator-based provisioning\n17\t    (operator layer available in time) or customer-supplied PostgreSQL, this epic\n18\t    is not implemented.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B)\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tConditional branch B of the provisioning decision: implemented when the go/no-go gate determines that the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (from the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) will not be available in time for RHOAI 3.5 and selects BFF-managed provisioning as the fallback. The odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly — similar to how it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure — creating the Kubernetes resources for the default vector store when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace. The implementation is designed for portability to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL expected in RHOAI 3.6, and gives explicit design attention to lifecycle concerns because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Implement BFF-managed provisioning: when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, persistent volume claim, and credentials Secret, using the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included — no custom image build needed).\n44\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning; the connection-consumption path is abstracted so this switch requires no user-facing change.\n45\t- Address lifecycle management explicitly, since the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, handling of PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation each get a documented design and implementation.\n46\t- Scope the provisioned instance per the confirmed scoping model (per-namespace is the preferred default for multi-tenant isolation).\n47\t- Set PostgreSQL resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's reference values, adjusted per the sprint-1 benchmark findings when available.\n48\t- End-to-end validation of the out-of-box experience: a playground with RAG configured gets a working default vector store with zero manual steps, and RAG workflows function with no loss of current capability with the remote pgvector provider enabled.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- A Gen AI Studio playground configured with RAG capabilities gets a working default pgvector vector store with zero manual provisioning or configuration steps by the user, via BFF-created Kubernetes resources.\n53\t- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and documented: resources are cleaned up on playground deletion, PostgreSQL pod restarts are handled, and credential rotation is designed and tested.\n54\t- The connection-consumption path is abstracted so a platform-provided shared connection string can replace self-provisioning without user-facing change (portability verified by a test that swaps the connection source).\n55\t- The provisioned PostgreSQL runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server instance, scoped per the confirmed model with per-namespace isolation.\n56\t- PostgreSQL resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference values and documented, with benchmark-driven adjustments noted.\n57\t- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function end-to-end with the remote pgvector vector_io provider enabled, with no loss of current capability.\n58\t- The provisioned instance's connection details are consumed by the playground's pgvector vector_io configuration via a Kubernetes secret.\n59\t\n60\t## HLR Traceability\n61\t\n62\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\" — this epic is the BFF-managed delivery of that capability under branch outcome B.\n63\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability\" — end-to-end validation included in this epic's scope.\n64\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider)\".\n65\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation)\".\n66\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is lightweight enough to run in resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead\" — resource requests/limits applied to the provisioned instance (bundled: the sizing is applied on the same provisioning surface this epic owns; splitting it would require a second pass over the same created resources).\n67\t- Staff Engineer Input: alternative fallback (BFF-managed provisioning following the guardrails-infrastructure pattern), portability caveat for the 3.6 shared database, lifecycle caveats (cleanup, restarts, credential rotation), existing pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL image, resource sizing reference.\n68\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8f8f198478e2793","agent_id":"a9f3dec29b468d8b4","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"03f90552-0185-4100-9ea0-2e2b889f0de7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:33:11.314Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8824374be6b0ff4f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
