{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a923642ffc025249","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n10\t\n11\t## Strategy Structure Notes\n12\t\n13\tThe strategy body states the full strategy exceeds Jira's description size limit and is stored as an attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`); that attachment is not present in the workspace. The Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, High Level Requirements, Dependencies table, and Non-Functional Requirements sections are therefore missing. The decomposition was derived from the Summary, Problem Statement, Acceptance Criteria, Open Questions, and the (extensive, non-empty) Staff Engineer Input. The Staff Engineer Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach; every override is logged below. See Health Warnings for the impact of the missing sections (notably: team assignments are inferred, and the HLR Traceability Matrix maps strategy Acceptance Criteria instead of P0/P1/P2 HLRs).\n14\t\n15\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n16\t\n17\tEach divergence between the Staff Engineer Input and the AI-generated strategy, as logged for traceability:\n18\t\n19\t1. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — OGX defaults to SQLite for application storage; the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite when creating OGXServer instances. Introducing PostgreSQL for vector storage is new work, not an extension of existing infrastructure.\n20\t2. **No custom image build, no Konflux onboarding** — `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector; the model-registry-operator already uses it as its auto-provisioned database image and `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is already in the rhods-operator CSV. The strategy's 1-2 sprint image onboarding estimate is eliminated; no onboarding-chain epics were generated.\n21\t3. **For RHOAI 3.5 Gen AI Studio must self-provision** — the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC will not be available until RHOAI 3.6.\n22\t4. **Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress)** — self-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern requires a RHOAI-controlled operator; the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic. Modeled as a gating Investigation with conditional branches (see DAG Justification).\n23\t5. **Fallbacks if RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time** — (a) customers bring their own PostgreSQL (OGXServer CR spec connection details), or (b) the gen-ai BFF provisions PostgreSQL directly (as it does guardrails infrastructure), with portability and lifecycle caveats. The development team defines a go/no-go gate as part of sprint planning. Modeled as bounded conditional branches.\n24\t6. **Auto Rag decoupled** — Auto Rag integration targets RHOAI 3.6 via RHAISTRAT-7824. Auto Rag is removed from affected components, impacted teams, acceptance criteria, and validation requirements. This strategy validates pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG workflows. Strategy acceptance criterion \"Auto Rag functions with the default vector store\" is therefore out of scope (logged in the HLR Traceability Matrix).\n25\t7. **Performance scope is dev experimentation** — no strict latency SLAs; the sprint-1 benchmark captures the inline Milvus baseline and verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude. Flat scan (no index) is sufficient for typical playground usage. Open questions about latency thresholds, corpus size ranges, and index type selection are removed.\n26\t8. **PostgreSQL resource sizing** — start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits; adjust based on sprint-1 benchmarking.\n27\t9. **Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations** — only the pgvector vector_io provider is configured; application storage stays on SQLite. No SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern; references to consolidating vector and application storage removed.\n28\t10. **Existing remote vector store implementation is reusable** — the majority of effort is infrastructure/provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector database, not the vector_io provider integration. The provider-integration epic is scoped accordingly.\n29\t\n30\t## Epic List\n31\t\n32\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n33\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Decide the default vector store provisioning approach for Gen AI Studio (go/no-go gate) | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground OGXServer instances | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set PostgreSQL resource sizing | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio (branch A) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004 | Customer-supplied PostgreSQL configuration support for Gen AI Studio RAG (branch C) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n40\t\n41\tBranch epics RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / -B- / -C- are conditional alternatives selected by the outcome of Investigation RHAISTRAT-8004-E001; exactly one is implemented. They are attached to the gating Investigation as a plan document, not created as standalone main-plan issues.\n42\t\n43\t## Dependency DAG\n44\t\n45\t```mermaid\n46\tgraph TD\n47\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E001<br/>Investigation: provisioning approach go/no-go\"]\n48\t    E002[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E002<br/>pgvector vector_io provider enablement\"]\n49\t    E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-E003<br/>benchmark + resource sizing\"]\n50\t    BA[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004<br/>operator-based provisioning (branch A)\"]\n51\t    BB[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004<br/>BFF-managed provisioning (branch B)\"]\n52\t    BC[\"RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004<br/>customer-supplied PostgreSQL (branch C)\"]\n53\t\n54\t    E001 --> BA\n55\t    E001 --> BB\n56\t    E001 --> BC\n57\t    E002 --> E003\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\tE001 and E002 are parallel roots (Tier 1 independent work). E003 runs after E002. Branch epics run after E001 resolves and in parallel with E002/E003.\n61\t\n62\t## DAG Justification\n63\t\n64\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n65\t|------|------|-----------|\n66\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | Whether the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (RHAISTRAT-7164) is available in time determines whether operator-based self-provisioning exists at all. True gate: `remove` if the outcome does not hold. |\n67\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E004 | Rule 3 | BFF-managed provisioning is selected only if the operator layer is not available and the go/no-go gate picks this fallback. True gate: `remove`. |\n68\t| E001 → BRANCH-C-E004 | Rule 3 | Customer-supplied PostgreSQL is the degraded-mode fallback, selected only if neither self-provisioning approach is viable for 3.5. True gate: `remove`. |\n69\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The benchmark exercises the pgvector provider integration delivered by E002; it builds on that capability, so it is serial. |\n70\t\n71\t**Deliberate non-edges (parallel work):**\n72\t\n73\t| Pair | Rule | Rationale |\n74\t|------|------|-----------|\n75\t| E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 14 / Step 5.5 Tier 1 | The vector_io provider enablement is needed under every provisioning outcome (operator-based, BFF-managed, or customer-supplied) and depends on no unresolved decision; it delivers independent partial value (Step 5.5 Tier 1). |\n76\t| E001 ∥ E003 | Step 5.5 Tier 1 | The benchmark stands up its own dev/test pgvector instance (dev experimentation scope) and does not need the production provisioning mechanism selected. |\n77\t| E002/E003 ∥ branch epics | Rule 13 (configuration reference, not build-against artifact) | The provisioning epics produce a connection string/secret that the provider configuration consumes at runtime — a configuration reference, expressed as an AC gate (\"the provisioned instance's connection details are consumed by the playground's pgvector vector_io configuration\"), never a DAG edge. Resource sizing flows from E003 to the branch epics the same way (reference values first, benchmark adjustment after). |\n78\t| main plan ⊥ branch epics | Branch-epic rule | No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in `dependencies`; anything depending on the provisioning outcome depends on E001. Each branch epic carries the end-to-end validation of the out-of-box experience for its own approach, so no separate cross-branch validation epic exists to depend on branch work. |\n79\t\n80\t**Epic boundary notes:** All active work centers on the Gen AI Studio playground stack (odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF plus, in branch A, the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer). ogx-distribution is a passive component here — its remote pgvector vector_io provider already ships (corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`), so no ogx-distribution code epic is generated; its validation is folded into E002's integration tests, E003's benchmark, and the branch epics' end-to-end ACs, all owned by the same team. rhods-operator needs no changes (`RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` already present per Staff Engineer Input). The go/no-go decision and the benchmark are separate epics because they are different work products (decision record vs. measurement artifact) resolved/produced by different activities, per the work-product-type boundary rule.\n81\t\n82\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n83\t\n84\tThe strategy has no High Level Requirements section (see Strategy Structure Notes); rows are the strategy's Acceptance Criteria plus Staff Engineer Input directives. \"SEI-n\" refers to the numbered overrides above.\n85\t\n86\t| Requirement | Priority basis | Epic(s) | Notes |\n87\t|-------------|----------------|---------|-------|\n88\t| AC-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration | Blocker | E001, E002, BRANCH-A-E004 (bundled), BRANCH-B-E004 (bundled) | BRANCH-C-E004 is the explicit degraded-mode exception where users supply PostgreSQL — that is the meaning of outcome C, not a coverage gap. E001 is bundled because the approach decision is a prerequisite of the same capability, not an independently deferrable feature: it shares the same deliverable surface (the out-of-box RAG experience) and splitting it from the provisioning work would serialize planning with no independent user value. |\n89\t| AC-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability | Blocker | E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004, BRANCH-C-E004 | E002 delivers the provider wiring; E003 verifies order-of-magnitude behavior vs. the inline Milvus baseline; each branch epic carries end-to-end no-loss-of-capability validation for its approach. |\n90\t| AC-3: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — | *(removed)* | Out of scope per SEI-6: Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector is addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Logged as an override, not an orphaned requirement. |\n91\t| AC-4: Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not inline) | Blocker | E002, BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004, BRANCH-C-E004 | Remote provider configuration (E002) plus a separately-running PostgreSQL process delivered by the provisioning work in each branch. |\n92\t| AC-5: Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation) | Blocker | E001 (bundled), BRANCH-A-E004, BRANCH-B-E004 | The scoping-model open question is resolved by E001 together with the approach decision — both are provisioning-design decisions made in the same planning activity and gating the same downstream epics, so they are combined into one Investigation rather than split. Branch epics implement the confirmed scoping with namespace isolation. |\n93\t| AC-6: Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | Blocker | E003, BRANCH-A-E004 (bundled), BRANCH-B-E004 (bundled) | E003 derives sizing from model-registry-operator reference values (SEI-8); the branch epics apply those requests/limits to the provisioned instance — tightly coupled to the same provisioning surface, so bundled rather than split. |\n94\t| SEI-4/SEI-5: go/no-go gate for the RHAISTRAT-7164 dependency and fallback selection | Blocker | E001 | The gate decision is the Investigation's deliverable. |\n95\t| SEI-9: vector storage independent of application storage (no migration) | Blocker | E002 | Captured as an explicit AC on E002. |\n96\t| SEI-7: benchmark scope (baseline capture, order-of-magnitude, flat scan, no thresholds) | Blocker | E003 | Defines E003's success criteria. |\n97\t\n98\t## Health Warnings\n99\t\n100\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLRs, Dependencies table, and NFRs are absent because the full strategy is in an unavailable attachment. Component and team assignments were inferred (see below); the traceability matrix maps Acceptance Criteria instead of HLRs. A human should verify against the attachment before execution.\n101\t- **Team assignments inferred**: no Impacted Teams table exists. All epics are assigned to \"Gen AI Studio\" based on the strategy label `dashboard-crimson-scrum`, the Staff Engineer Input's references to the gen-ai BFF and the Gen AI Studio lead, and the removal of Auto Rag from scope. The branch-A operator work lands in the operator layer delivered by RHAISTRAT-7164 — confirm the owning team with that strategy's stakeholders.\n102\t- **Component mapping note**: all epics use the canonical component `Gen AI Studio` (the closest canonical name for the Gen AI Studio playground stack). The work physically spans the odh-dashboard repository's gen-ai BFF (E002, branch B, branch C), the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer (branch A), and validation against the ogx-distribution's existing remote pgvector provider (passive, no code changes). ogx-distribution and rhods-operator require no code changes.\n103\t- **Externally-controlled schedule dependency**: whether RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for RHOAI 3.5 is outside this strategy's control. The branch structure isolates this risk, but the go/no-go decision date is a schedule risk, not something the epics can compress. Not filed as an AC anywhere.\n104\t- **Unresolved open question — migration path to a production-grade vector database** (raised by Bram Sundqvist): the Staff Engineer Input does not resolve it. It does not change which epics exist in this strategy — the OGX provider-plugin architecture already allows switching remote vector backends by configuration, and the default store is positioned for dev experimentation with production-scale users directed to external vector databases (captured in E003's report and the branch epics' positioning). If product later decides migration tooling is in scope, that is net-new work beyond this decomposition.\n105\t- **Priority note**: E003 is P1 — it validates the \"lightweight / no loss of capability\" acceptance criteria and informs sizing, but the out-of-box capability is delivered by E001/E002 plus the selected branch epic, and sizing starts from the model-registry-operator reference values without waiting for the benchmark. Nothing P0 depends on E003.\n106\t\n107\t## Tiered Delivery\n108\t\n109\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing) has uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n110\t\n111\t- **Tier 1 (independent of RHAISTRAT-7164)**: E002 (pgvector vector_io provider enablement — needed under every outcome) and E003 (benchmark and resource sizing — runs against a dev/test instance). These proceed immediately and in parallel with the go/no-go decision.\n112\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: the provisioning branch epics (BRANCH-A/B/C-E004), gated by E001's resolution of the RHAISTRAT-7164 timing question. Conditional decomposition with three bounded outcomes:\n113\t  - **Branch A** — RHAISTRAT-7164 delivers the RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time: operator-based self-provisioning following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n114\t  - **Branch B** — operator layer not available: gen-ai BFF manages the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (guardrails-infrastructure pattern), designed for portability to the 3.6 platform-provided shared database.\n115\t  - **Branch C** — neither self-provisioning approach viable for 3.5: customers bring their own PostgreSQL via OGXServer CR spec configuration (degraded mode; aligns with OGX 1.0 breaking changes).\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3e7679c48fa5da5","agent_id":"a84b1f058f9da17e6","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"c38bdcfb-ffbb-488d-91f1-d0f3c125162e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:37:19.711Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a923642ffc025249","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
