{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0f6a4a51a3f2e7f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: Decide the default vector store provisioning approach for Gen AI Studio (go/no-go\n4\t  gate)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 0\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -1\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Decide the default vector store provisioning approach for Gen AI Studio (go/no-go gate)\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves how the default pgvector-backed vector store is provisioned for the Gen AI Studio playground in RHOAI 3.5, and confirms the scoping model for that store. It produces a decision record that selects one of three bounded provisioning outcomes and defines the go/no-go gate criteria, decision date, and owner as part of sprint planning. The downstream provisioning implementation is conditional on this decision: which implementation exists, and what it does, is determined by the outcome.\n31\t\n32\t## Scope\n33\t\n34\t- Track and verify the delivery status of the dashboard operator modularization effort (RHAISTRAT-7164) against the RHOAI 3.5 release timeline. This is the hard dependency for operator-based self-provisioning: the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic, so until the modularization delivers a RHOAI-controlled operator layer, there is no home for PostgreSQL lifecycle management.\n35\t- Define the go/no-go gate: the criteria, the decision date, and the owner, as part of sprint planning.\n36\t- Select the provisioning approach among the bounded outcomes:\n37\t  - **A — operator-based self-provisioning**: PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled Gen AI Studio operator layer, following the model-registry-operator pattern (viable only if the operator layer lands in time).\n38\t  - **B — BFF-managed provisioning**: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (as it currently provisions guardrails infrastructure), with portability and lifecycle design attention.\n39\t  - **C — customer-supplied PostgreSQL**: users configure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details (degraded mode aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes).\n40\t- Confirm the scoping model for the default vector store — per namespace versus per OGXServer instance — noting that per-namespace is preferred for isolation and that the per-instance consumer (Auto Rag) is out of scope for this strategy (its integration is targeted at RHOAI 3.6 under RHAISTRAT-7824).\n41\t- Record the decision, rationale, and gate outcome in a decision record consumed by sprint planning; the conditional implementation branches for each outcome are attached to this epic as a plan document.\n42\t\n43\t## Acceptance Criteria\n44\t\n45\t- A decision record exists that names the selected provisioning approach, the go/no-go gate criteria, the decision date, and the owner.\n46\t- The decision is grounded in the verified delivery status of the dashboard operator modularization against the RHOAI 3.5 timeline, not in assumption.\n47\t- The scoping model (per-namespace or per-OGXServer-instance) is confirmed with rationale, consistent with multi-tenant isolation.\n48\t- Each bounded outcome identifies the implementation work it selects, so the conditional branches are unambiguous.\n49\t- The decision is socialized with sprint planning and the Gen AI Studio stakeholders.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\" — this epic is the prerequisite decision that determines how that capability is delivered (bundled, not independently deferrable: it shares the same deliverable surface and has no standalone user value).\n54\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation)\" — the scoping model is resolved here.\n55\t- Strategy open question \"What is the preferred scoping model — per namespace, per Llama Stack instance, or both?\" — explicitly resolved by this epic.\n56\t- Staff Engineer Input: operator dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164, fallback definition, and the directive that the development team define a go/no-go gate as part of sprint planning.\n57\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-988303e1dab42617","agent_id":"ab2a1f821930acc44","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"adb737db-a15f-4cf4-9a01-e2d52a75cdaf","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:40:35.686Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0f6a4a51a3f2e7f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
