{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99c770df25cb8859","type":"tool_result","content":".context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ai4rag.md\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/odh-dashboard.md\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md\n---odh-dashboard guardrails---\n**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\nThe odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n| gen-ai BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated gen-ai backend-for-frontend service |\n| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/gen-ai/bff/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| /gen-ai/api/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n---ogx-k8s secrets---\n**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages OGX inference servers on RHOAI, reconciling OGXServer custom resources into fully configured deployments with autoscaling, network policies, monitoring, and a validating admission webhook. [source: main.go:101, api/v1beta1/ogxserver_webhook.go:54, config/rbac/role.yaml:2]\n**Detailed**: ogx-k8s-operator ships two controller-runtime binaries. The root `ogx-k8s-operator` binary runs the OGXServerReconciler, which watches OGXServer CRs (ogx.io/v1beta1) and reconciles each into a Deployment, Service, HPA, PDB, NetworkPolicy, ConfigMap, and optional Ingress and PersistentVolumeClaim. It label-filters its informer caches to only track resources with `app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ogx-operator` and applies the cluster's OpenShift TLS profile (with an Intermediate fallback on non-OpenShift clusters) to the webhook server. The `ogx-module` binary is a separate controller-runtime operator that manages the OGX platform component CRD (components.platform.opendatahub.io/OGX) and uses odh-platform-utilities for kustomize-based manifest rendering, condition management, garbage collection, and platform detection. A `configgen` CLI generates runtime configuration from distribution specifications. A validating webhook (`vogxserver.kb.io`) enforces OGXServer schema integrity on CREATE and UPDATE operations with a Fail failure policy. [source: main.go:101-106, main.go:108-136, main.go:156-198, ogx-module/internal/controller/ogx/reconciler.go:15-20, config/webhook/manifests.yaml:2-27, Dockerfile.konflux:29]\nogx-k8s-operator follows a dual-binary operator pattern common in the RHOAI platform. The root operator (`ogx-k8s-operator`) owns the user-facing OGXServer CR lifecycle, while `ogx-module` manages the platform-level OGX component CRD that the RHOAI operator uses to install and configure OGX itself. This separation keeps the user-facing reconciliation loop independent of the platform bootstrapping logic.\nThe OGXServerReconciler owns a broad set of child resources—Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, HPAs, PDBs, NetworkPolicies, Ingresses, PVCs, and Secrets—and uses label-scoped informer caches (`app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: ogx-operator`) to reduce watch pressure on the API server. It also watches Secrets and ConfigMaps with a dedicated label selector rather than listing the entire namespace, which is a meaningful isolation boundary in multi-tenant clusters. A legacy adoption path exists for migrating pre-existing resources under operator management (controllers/legacy_adoption.go).\nThe ogx-module controller relies heavily on odh-platform-utilities for kustomize-based manifest rendering, controller condition helpers, garbage collection, and platform detection (ODH vs RHOAI). It manages ValidatingWebhookConfigurations, CRDs, RBAC resources, and Deployments as owned resources, providing full lifecycle management for the root operator's installation. Prometheus monitoring integration is established via RBAC grants on monitoring.coreos.com PrometheusRules and ServiceMonitors. [source: main.go:108-136, main.go:156-198, ogx-module/internal/controller/ogx/reconciler.go:15-20, config/rbac/role.yaml:2]\n| OGX controller | Controller | Reconciles OGX resources |\n| OGXServer controller | Controller | Reconciles OGXServer resources |\n| ogx-k8s-operator-manager-role |  | secrets | get, list, watch |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-988303e1dab42617","agent_id":"ab2a1f821930acc44","session_id":"c797c45b-eabe-4faf-a684-66c1833f1941","uuid":"6002dbef-e086-4011-bd3a-d19bd4c5ef24","timestamp":"2026-10-26T12:41:44.899Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-99c770df25cb8859","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
